NOTE ON OpenBSD netcat / POCHODZENIE KODU

This project is an independent, from-scratch reimplementation written for
educational purposes. Below is exactly what came from where.

WHAT IS ORIGINAL TO THIS REPOSITORY (covered by the MIT license above)

  * All source code, in both languages:
      - pyncat/*.py            (Python package, ~855 lines of code)
      - c_reference/pyncat_ref.c (C11 reference implementation, ~859 lines)
      - tests/*.py, ci/*, web/*
    Every line was written for this project. The C file in particular is NOT
    a copy, a translation, or a reformatting of netcat.c -- it was written
    from scratch against the same specification, which is why its function
    bodies, error handling, helper functions (nc_strtonum, port_strdup,
    free_ports, set_deadline, ms_left) and comments have no counterpart in
    the original.
  * All documentation in docs/ and README.md.
  * All empirical measurements: throughput, startup overhead, syscall counts,
    RSS, the BUFSIZE sweep, and the behavioural tables. These were produced by
    running the code on the authors' machine, not taken from any other source.

WHAT WAS REPRODUCED FROM ANALYSIS OF netcat.c (not copied)

  * Algorithms and semantics:
      - the four-slot pollfd model of readwrite(), in which POLL_NETOUT and
        POLL_NETIN are two entries over the same descriptor;
      - the three loop-exit conditions and the EOF propagation rules;
      - the POLLHUP-with-POLLIN rule for not closing an input that still has
        buffered data;
      - the backpressure pattern in fillbuf()/drainbuf();
      - the exit-code decision table, including the "once zero, always zero"
        behaviour of the port loop;
      - the four-write mechanism of udptest();
      - the three listen-mode variants for TCP, UDP, and UDP with -k.
  * Constants: BUFSIZE 16384, PORT_MAX 65535, the POLL_* slot indices,
    listen() backlog of 1, the 2048-byte MSG_PEEK buffer.
  * Flag semantics and the wording of a small number of diagnostic strings
    that are part of the reproduced behaviour ("port number too large",
    "service \"%s\" unknown", and the strtonum(3) error words "invalid",
    "too small", "too large").

  These are documented throughout the source with references to the
  originating function names and line numbers in
  $OpenBSD: netcat.c,v 1.241 2026/10/31 03:42:10 tb Exp $
  and collected in docs/netcat-architecture.md.

WHAT IS NOT HERE

  No netcat source code has been copied into this repository as code, and the
  file netcat.c itself is not redistributed here. docs/netcat-architecture.md
  explains how to fetch it from upstream in order to verify any of the line
  references.

  To be exact about the one place where upstream text does appear: the
  architecture document quotes, as marked block quotations with attribution,
  a single three-line source comment (the author's own note on the -i flag,
  netcat.c:1149-1151) and a handful of sentences from the nc.1 manual page.
  They are quoted because in those sections the wording itself is what is
  being analysed -- the point is precisely that the documentation and the
  code disagree. No C code is quoted anywhere, and nothing from netcat is
  compiled into, imported by, or shipped as part of this project.

UPSTREAM LICENSING

  OpenBSD's nc(1) (usr.bin/nc/netcat.c) is distributed under a 3-clause BSD
  license:
      Copyright (c) 2001 Eric Jackson <ericj@monkey.org>
      Copyright (c) 2015 Bob Beck.  All rights reserved.
  The original netcat (1.10, 1996) was written by *Hobbit*
  <hobbit@avian.org> and released into the public domain.

  The -q flag reproduced here does not exist in OpenBSD upstream; it comes
  from the Debian netcat-openbsd package's patch set. Its semantics were
  established by measuring the installed binary (netcat-openbsd
  1.226-1ubuntu2), because the patch source was not reachable from the
  build environment -- see docs/tablice-decyzyjne.md section 9.4.

  This project is not affiliated with, nor endorsed by, the OpenBSD project
  or the Debian netcat-openbsd maintainers.

THIRD-PARTY TEST AND DEMO DEPENDENCIES

  Not vendored; fetched at build/test time and each covered by its own
  license: pytest and ruff (MIT), jsdom (MIT), Pyodide (MPL-2.0). The demo
  page loads Pyodide from a CDN at runtime. The container images used by the
  network test (nginx, redis, python:alpine) are pulled from Docker Hub by CI
  and are not part of this repository.
